          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-f8f4b1a45a0f8e15\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Rafa’s direct “Ti amo” is principally an avowal that he loves Loretta. Its repetition and surrounding claims of long-standing, increasing, and exclusive love strongly support T. Because it is used while pressing a concrete proposal and seeking her assent, it has slight commitment-invoking force, but the undertaking is chiefly carried by the surrounding promises and plans rather than by “Ti amo” itself. The passionate, supplicatory delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited uncertainty over P: the declaration reassures Loretta of his intended exclusivity, but it does not by itself clearly constitute a promise or relational commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rafa directly addresses Loretta with the first “Ti amo” in the repeated phrase “Ti amo, ti amo in tutti i modi.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rafa presents the declaration as evidence that Loretta is unique and that his proposed arrangement will not be temporary or serial. It occurs within an ardent attempt to obtain her assent, accompanied by promises, pleading, physical pressure, and disregard of her requests to be released. This gives the avowal a persuasive and potentially manipulative role without displacing its truth-conditional mode; the passage may portray his desire as genuine even while his conduct is coercive.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ti amo, ti amo in tutti i modi” (“I love you, I love you in every way”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense declaration and its elaboration strongly support truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Rafa says that he has loved her “da lunghi mesi, ogni giorno più forte” (“for many months, more strongly every day”).\",\"supports\":\"The declaration is framed as the report of an enduring and intensifying emotional state, rather than as a merely reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rafa contrasts Loretta with prior women—“Non erano te” and “Non ho avuto nessun amore, prima di conoscere te”—while offering to change houses and planning a future arrangement.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance reassures her of uniqueness and supports slight commitment-invoking force in the immediate courtship proposal.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He speaks on his knees with passionate eyes and a pleading voice, then continues physical advances while she asks him to stop and says he is hurting her.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration operates in an intensely persuasive and coercively pressured encounter, but these delivery and contextual features do not independently support E or O.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the core avowal, with a small allowance for commitment-invoking use under P. Passion, persuasion, and coercive context can be described contextually without requiring E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
